"In the first verse of Surah al-Mulk, Allah's majesty and the fact that everything is in His dominion are emphasized. He possesses eternal perfection and is supreme above all things with His endless goodness and blessings. Dominion (mulk)—that is, disposal, creation, annihilation, governance, and punishment in the earth and the heavens, in this world and the Hereafter—is entirely in His hand of power. He gives dominion to whom He wills and takes it away from whom He wills; He has no partner and is powerful over all things.\n\nIn the second verse, the wisdom behind Allah's creation of death and life is explained: to test people and reveal which of them will do better deeds. Here, it is understood that death, like life, is a created entity. The majority of Ahl al-Sunnah scholars accept that death is not mere non-existence, but an occurrence pertaining to existence. Death is the opposite of life and a transition carrying human beings to another realm.\n\nThe mention of death before life reminds us that man came into life from non-existence, and will eventually die again and be resurrected. This emphasizes that the life of this world is transient and that the true life is the Hereafter. Death is a punishment for the disbelievers, whereas for the believers it is a gate of salvation and grace. The verse encourages people toward good deeds and reminds them of the recompense in the Hereafter.\n\nAt the conclusion of the test, it is declared that Allah is the Almighty (al-Aziz) and the All-Forgiving (al-Ghafur). He vanquishes those who do evil, elevates those who do good, and forgives those who repent. This verse shows that the purpose of the creation of human beings is to worship Allah and to know Him. This is also in harmony with the verse in Surah adh-Dhariyat: 'I did not create the jinn and mankind except to worship Me.'\n\nIn the third verse, the creation of the seven heavens is described. The number 'seven' can express both a specific count and multiplicity. These heavens encompass the orbits of the planets in the solar system and the realm of stars beyond them. The seven heavens are layers that can be counted when viewed from our earth; these layers are conceived as nested concentric circles.\n\nAccording to modern astronomy, our earth is the first heaven, Mars (Merih) the second, Jupiter (Mushtari) the third, Saturn (Zuhal) the fourth, Uranus the fifth, Neptune the sixth, and beyond the Milky Way is the seventh heaven. This order demonstrates Allah's power and the order of the universe. The creation of the seven heavens and the earth reveals the magnificence of Allah's knowledge and power.\n\nIn Allah's dominion, the number seven holds great significance. For the concept of a physical body (jism), seven points must come together. The earth and sky are conceived as seven through the six directions and one central point. This is expressed in the verse: 'So turn your vision again: can you see any flaw?' (al-Mulk, 67:3). Through material sight, seven heavens are seen, but what lies beyond is grasped through reason and revelation.\n\nFrom a spiritual perspective, there are seven windows in the human heart: the five senses, intellect and logic, inspiration and revelation. The spirit ascends and descends through these windows. Satan and the jinn, on the other hand, are evil forces that attempt to obstruct these spiritual currents. Through this realm of seven senses, human beings attain truth and attain faith.\n\nBeyond the material heavens, there are spiritual heavens. Sidrat al-Muntaha (the Lote Tree of the Furthest Boundary) is the ultimate point situated in the sixth and seventh heavens. In the hadiths of the Ascension (Mi'raj), it is related that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) crossed the seven heavens both bodily and spiritually and reached the presence of Allah. This was an ascension accomplished with both body and soul.\n\nAllah's Essence (Dhat) is transcendent above both the material and spiritual realms. Some look solely from a material viewpoint and imagine Allah as a physical body, while others look from a spiritual viewpoint and suppose Him to be a universal spirit. Both views are false. Allah's Essence encompasses all things and is exalted above everything. In His dominion, spirit and body are like two wings that testify to His existence.\n\nThe concept of a physical body is not limited to three dimensions. Four-, five-, six-, and seven-dimensional realms can be conceived. In this way, our realm may be envisioned as a point, and the other realms as higher dimensions. However, this line of thought carries the risk of causing one to lose oneself. The primary purpose is to grasp Allah's oneness and attain the truth.\n\nThese verses invite mankind to reflection (tafakkur) upon Allah's dominion. The harmony between the material and spiritual realms demonstrates Allah's power. Seeing the order in these realms, man ought to strengthen his faith and act with the consciousness of servitude. Indeed, in the continuation of Surah al-Mulk, Allah's mercy and power are recounted.\n\nElmalılı explains that moving beyond the three-dimensional physical realm is a mental exercise, and that in this way one delves into the spiritual realm. He states that the notion of a four-dimensional body consists merely of spiritual dimensions, and that in reality one leaves the physical realm and passes into the realm of imagination. He emphasizes that rather than limiting or increasing the conception of physical bodies to reach the Truth, rising to the encompassment of both realms is a firmer path.\n\nDwelling on whether a four-dimensional physical realm is possible, Elmalılı states that it may be hypothesized and imagined, but cannot be judged to exist in reality. Nevertheless, he expresses that this supposition is beneficial in prompting those accustomed to the physical realm to reflect on the expanse of the spiritual realm and the vastness of Allah's dominion. He adds that for consciousness to surrender to its Creator, it is sufficient for a person to be sensible of oneself and one's creation.\n\nHe notes that those who are conscious of their consciousness see the proof of the Creator in every consciousness and attain the secret of the verse: 'He is with you wherever you are' (al-Hadid, 57:4). He states that believers strive to increase their knowledge and deeds by recognizing the blessings in Allah's dominion, that understanding the physical realm in three dimensions is sufficient, and that upon stepping out of it, one enters the realm of spirituality. He emphasizes that it is possible to conceive the seven heavens as being in mutual conformity or layer upon layer, but that Allah's creation of seven heavens is certain.\n\nHe explains that the word tibaq can be a verbal noun (masdar) or a plural, and that most exegetes have preferred the meaning of mutabaqah (conformity). He explains with examples that the meaning 'layer upon layer' is also fitting, and that the planets and realms of perception exist in layers. In commenting on the verse, 'You see no disparity in the creation of the Most Merciful,' he indicates that tafawut means disparity and lack of order, and that Allah created the heavens in harmony as a manifestation of His mercy.\n\nExplaining the word futur (fissure/cleft), Elmalılı indicates that the verse is not proof that the heaven cannot be cleaved; rather, cleaving is indeed possible, but Allah's power encompasses all things. Regarding the turning back of the gaze, he emphasizes the necessity of looking repeatedly, noting that the two perceptions of the eye are one. He gives the meanings of the words khasi'an and hasir: the former means driven away in humiliation or dazzled, while the latter means fatigued and exhausted. Through these expressions, he states that the gaze returns in bewilderment and impotence without being able to find any flaw in dominion, and that in this way a person will come to understand Allah's oneness and power and believe.\n\nAddressing the subject of the lowest heaven (the nearest heaven), Elmalılı states that this expression is an adjectival phrase and that 'dunya' can mean the lowest or nearest heaven. He explains that it is the first of the seven heavens, beginning from the lunar orbit, but because it is adorned with stars, some commentators have interpreted this more broadly. Quoting the view of Baha' al-Din al-Amili, he notes that the claim that the stars are gathered within the lunar orbit is not necessary, and that meteors (shihabs / flaming projectiles) can fall from the stars. He expresses that the qualification 'dunya' in the verse does not mean that the lamps (masabih) belong solely to the first heaven, but perhaps points generally to the visible stars.\n\nElmalılı states that there are two views regarding what the lowest heaven is. The first view is that the lowest heaven is the lowest tier, being one of the seven heavens. The second view is that the lowest heaven is the celestial sphere seen from the earth that encompasses all the stars. Elmalılı says that the second view, preferred by verifying scholars, is more accurate. According to him, the lowest heaven is the inner surface of a sphere—what we call the sky—that accommodates everything we see within it, whose outer surface is known only to Allah. This view is compatible both with the apparent meaning of the ayah and with the hadiths.\n\nCiting al-Alusi, he explains that the word 'masabih' is the plural of 'misbah', meaning lamp. It is debated whether calling stars lamps is metaphorical or literal. Elmalılı argues that calling stars lamps in terms of giving light is not metaphorical, and that they can be regarded as literal lamps. He also states that 'masabih' being brought in the indefinite form is for magnification (ta'zim)—that is, ordinary lamps are not intended, but great and precious lamps. He emphasizes that these lamps are stars that illuminate the surroundings at night like lanterns.\n\nComparing the views of ancient and modern astronomers, Elmalılı explains that ancient astronomers conceived of the heavens as solid spheres, whereas modern ones view them as bodies moving in space through gravitational forces. He expresses that Quranic verses (for instance, Ya-Sin 36/40, Ar-Rahman 55/7, and Ar-Ra'd 13/2) are understood more easily through modern astronomy, and therefore it is possible to understand the verses in their apparent meanings without needing strained interpretations. However, he adds that no science or philosophy can fully comprehend the sublime truths pointed to by the Quran, and that it is not right to restrict tafsir to the knowledge of a specific era.\n\nStating that the sources of human knowledge are the senses, reason, and true report, Elmalılı says that the senses do not deceive within their own domain, and that errors arise in reasoning when attributing things to causes beyond sensation. He gives the example of a ship passenger who supposes the shore to be moving; in reality, it is the ship that is moving, but through an error of reason, the shore is assumed to move. Similarly, he emphasizes that when interpreting the movements of celestial bodies, it may not be our senses, but our ideas that are mistaken. He explains that the visible sky is a domain that encompasses our senses, and that beyond this domain can be reached only through faith.\n\nElmalılı renders the meaning of the verse as follows: 'By My Majesty, We adorned that lowest heaven with material and spiritual lamps that delight eyes and hearts, and We made them missiles against devils.' Here, he notes that lamps can be understood both as material sources of light (stars) and as spiritual lights (knowledge and faith). He says that the primary purpose is for these lamps to awaken faith in hearts by pointing to divine power and beauty. He recalls that the matter of shooting at devils is also related to this spiritual dimension, and that stars are used to drive devils away.\n\nIn this section, two main meanings of the word 'rujum' are emphasized first. The first meaning signifies physical projectiles cast from the sky to drive devils away, namely flaming fires (shihabs). This is supported by verses found in Surah al-Hijr and Surah as-Saffat. Accordingly, devils cannot listen to the exalted assembly of angels; yet if they attempt to eavesdrop, they are pelted and driven away by a flame of fire that pursues them. What is important here is that it is noted that while this physical occurrence may be connected to spiritual events, this physical meaning is not the interpretation most fitting to the apparent meaning of the ayah.\n\nThe second meaning is that 'rujum' is a spiritual stoning. That is, the stars serve as spiritual projectiles against devils. Prophets, Companions, and scholars are likened to these spiritual projectiles. As also stated in Surah al-Jinn, after the Prophet (peace be upon him) was sent, jinn and devils could no longer approach the heaven. In this interpretation, devils encompass both devils among jinn and devils among humans. Human devils are those who deceive the public by casting stones into the unseen, such as astrologers and soothsayers. The verse encompasses both meanings; in the first, jinn devils are described, and in the second, human devils.\n\nIn this context, astrology (ahkam al-nujum), which attempts to derive decrees from the stars, is severely criticized. As Ibn Sina also stated, knowing the unseen belongs exclusively to Allah. It is not possible to derive definitive judgments concerning the future from the positions of stars, because events are formed not merely through celestial factors, but through the combination of many factors on earth. For this reason, the claims of astrologers consist mostly of falsehood. Islamic scholars have reached consensus that believing in the influence of the stars is disbelief and polytheism.\n\nThen the verse describes the condition of the disbelievers in Hell. When they are cast into Hell, they hear its terrifying roar. Hell is almost bursting with rage. As each group is cast in, the keeper-angels (zabaniyah) ask them sternly: 'Did no warner come to you?' The disbelievers then confess the truth: 'Yes, a warner came to us, but we denied him and said, \"Allah has not sent down anything; you are in nothing but great error.\"' Thus, they confess their own error.\n\nThis confession reveals the true nature of disbelief: not only denying the existence of Allah, but also not believing in His prophets, His scriptures, and His miracles is disbelief. The disbelievers, paying no heed to the warnings, condemned themselves to eternal punishment. Here, it is stated that those who say 'Allah has not sent down anything' are in fact deists who deny prophethood. The Quran is more exalted and protected than all divine scriptures; it has suffered no corruption whatsoever.\n\nThe polytheists characterized the Quran, which is the word of Allah, as a fabrication of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). Yet the Quran rejected these claims of theirs and demanded that they produce the like of it. Even Western writers have admitted that the Prophet (peace be upon him) could not have founded such a moral and revolutionary religion. In his tafsir, Razi states that there are two possibilities regarding whether the expression 'You are in nothing but great error' is a word spoken by the disbelievers to the prophets or a word that the keepers of Hell will say to them. According to the preferred view, this is the word of the disbelievers, and 'dalal' denotes their error in this world or the destruction that is its consequence.\n\nWhen the disbelievers are interrogated in Hellfire, they say: If only we had listened or reasoned, we would not have suffered punishment. According to them, there were three reasons for their falling into disbelief: not listening, not using their intellect, and being in a bad environment. Hearing and intellect are man's two most precious guides; those who do not use them are lower than animals. However, those who lack any capacity whatsoever are not held accountable. As for the disbelievers, because they possessed hearing and intellect yet failed to use them, they deserve the punishment.\n\nThe disbelievers confess their sins and realize that there is no deficiency in Allah's grace or justice. Allah has adorned the lowest heaven with lamps, sent down stars and verses, and tested people with glad tidings and warnings. (In verse 12) For those who revere their Lord unseen—that is, who guard themselves in sincere fear—there is forgiveness and a great reward. Those who do not fear until they see the punishment, or those who are hypocrites, are deprived of this.\n\nWhether speech is secret or open makes no difference to Allah; He knows it all. He knows the innermost essence of all breasts. Does He who created not know? He is al-Latif (the Subtle, Most Kind) and al-Khabir (the All-Aware). Latif means exceedingly gracious or subtle; Khabir means aware of all things. According to al-Razi, these attributes belong to the Creator, and His creation is realized through knowledge and power. Created beings have nothing that is concealed from Him.\n\nAllah made the earth subservient (zalul) for mankind. It is commanded to walk upon its shoulders (manakib). Commentators have offered different views regarding manakib: according to Qatadah, mountains and hills; according to al-Hasan, roads and sides; and according to al-Zamakhshari, this expression is a metonymy for the earth being so docile that it can be trodden like the shoulders of a camel. According to the most comprehensive view, manakib encompasses all parts of the earth—east, west, north, and south. This ayah encourages Muslims to study geography and other earth sciences and to make explorations.\n\nThe commands 'walk' and 'eat' in Surah al-Mulk, verse 15, have generally been interpreted by commentators as indicating permissibility (mubah). However, Elmalılı states that these commands are not merely permissible, but also convey a divine grace and a warning. When a person must secure their livelihood and eat enough to stay alive, these commands take on the status of an obligation (wajib). Moreover, the purpose of these actions is also essential; one must act without forgetting Allah, mindful of being called to account, and staying away from evil intentions. In this respect, the verse serves as both an individual and a social warning.\n\nThe commands 'walk' and 'eat' in the same verse encompass all kinds of beneficial endeavor in the struggle of life, such as working and earning, jihad, commerce, and agriculture. They also include such objectives as traveling the earth to take heed, acquiring knowledge, and seeking spiritual sustenance. These commands are primarily directed toward society, encouraging Muslims to hasten toward good deeds and to perform the best of works in the arena of trial. The word 'provision' in the phrase 'eat of His provision' encompasses not only material sustenance, but also spiritual blessings such as knowledge, pointing to the connection between the earth and the heavens.\n\nThere are three views concerning 'manakib' (shoulders) in the phrase 'walk upon its shoulders': the first is mountains and hills; the second is paths and sides; and the third is a metonymy for extreme submissiveness, as the most compliant part of a mount. Elmalılı states that the most comprehensive interpretation is 'all regions of the earth', which covers every direction—east, west, north, and south. In addition, this verse encourages the pursuit of sciences such as geography and the earth sciences.\n\nAllah's making the earth 'zalul' (subservient) indicates that it is readily usable like a docile mount. 'Zalul' is distinct from 'zalil'; being zalul does not imply being humiliated or abased. Walking upon the shoulders of the earth expresses both physically walking upon the earth and advancing toward the sky by means of transport. This is a journey in which worldly and otherworldly purposes converge. In this journey, the believer must seek the pleasure of Allah and never forget that the final return is unto Him.\n\nThe expression 'Do you feel secure that He who is in the heaven...?' in verse 16 is a matter of discussion. While some maintain this refers to the angels, the majority hold the view that it refers to Allah. However, since Allah is free from any need for a physical body or a place, the expression 'the heaven' denotes absolute transcendence and supremacy. The scholars of the Salaf regarded this verse as mutashabih (allegorical) and did not venture into figurative interpretation (ta'wil), consigning its ultimate reality to Allah. Elmalılı notes that 'heaven' here does not signify a material body, but rather refers to the Exalted Being who is above all things.\n\nThe transcendence of Allah means that He is supreme over all things and nothing is like unto Him. The statement 'Allah is in heaven' indicates not that He is in need of space, but rather His absolute transcendence and that He is omnipresent and omniscient. This is in accord with the verse: 'He is the only God in the heavens and on earth' (al-An'am 6:3). Saying 'Allah is on earth,' however, is not permissible because it implies lowliness; whereas saying 'Allah is in heaven' is correct in the sense of grandeur and highness. Even so, it must not be understood that His Essence is contained within the heavens and the earth; He is the deity worshipped in the heavens and on the earth.\n\nIn verses 16–17, those who defy Allah's power are warned: Are the people walking upon the earth secure from His punishment? Allah could destroy them by shaking the earth beneath them or by sending a storm of stones upon them. Earlier nations (Noah, 'Ad, Thamud, Pharaoh, etc.) denied the messengers and suffered a terrible punishment. This is a lesson and warning for the deniers.\n\nIn verse 19, the state of the birds flying above them is brought to attention: none holds them as they fly with wings outspread and folded except the Most Merciful (al-Rahman). They move according to Allah's ordained system. In the same way, human beings, as they walk upon the earth, remain upright only by Allah's power. Lessons must be drawn from the birds, and the commands of Allah must be obeyed. No matter how high or far they fly, they cannot escape His forces.\n\nIn verse 21, the question is asked: 'Who is it that can provide for you if He withholds His provision?' If Allah were to cut off provision, no one could provide water, air, or food. Therefore, we must recognize that provision comes only from Allah, and we must work while offering gratitude to Him. The disbelievers, on the other hand, flee from the truth, veering away sideways in obstinacy.\n\nIn verse 22, the condition of one who walks fallen prone on his face is compared with that of one who walks upright on a straight path. Those who crawl face-down look only toward baseness and deviate into falsehood; those who walk upright follow the path of tawhid (divine unity). Man should not crawl like an animal; rather, he ought to walk on the straight path, within the bounds of morality and the Shariah, and build up and cultivate the earth.\n\nIn verse 23, it is emphasized that Allah has granted human beings hearing, sight, and hearts, and that they must listen, see, and reason with them. Sadly, people show little gratitude for these blessings and use them wrongfully. Yet these faculties were bestowed specifically to perceive Allah's warnings and to find the right path.\n\nAllah has granted human beings senses such as hearing, sight, and hearts. These are His warning messengers. Through these faculties, people see, hear, and reflect upon Allah's signs. Most people, however, fail to fulfill their gratitude for these blessings and use them on wrongful paths. Had they listened and reasoned, they would have been saved from the punishment of Hell. Ears and eyes warn a person by signaling dangers from afar, whereas senses like touch and taste only perceive what is immediate, at which point it is already too late to protect oneself.\n\nAllah is the One Who created people and spread them across the earth. He settles each of you in a place, multiplies you, and scatters you. In the end, all of you will be gathered into His presence. This promise of gathering is certain. Why, then, do you worship others and not turn to Him? If you were to heed His tidings and obey, you would understand that you cannot escape death and that you are in need of Him at every moment. It is then that you would turn your face to Him, fear Him alone, and be grateful to Him.\n\nThe disbelievers ask, 'When will this promise of gathering be fulfilled?' O Muhammad! Say: The knowledge of it is only with Allah. I am only a clear warner. Knowing its appointed time does not belong to me. I am only informing you that it will happen. This warning is to caution against the punishment of the Hereafter.\n\nSay: Whether Allah destroys me and the believers with me or has mercy on us and grants us life, in either case there is none who can save the disbelievers from a painful punishment. As long as you do not believe, the death of the Prophet will bring you no benefit. The punishment is due to your disbelief and ingratitude. As for the believers, they do not fear death; they depart gladly to eternal life.\n\nSay: The One Who grants us mercy is the Most Merciful (al-Rahman). We have believed in Him and in Him alone have we put our trust. Soon you will know who it is that is in clear misguidance. Consider: If your water were to sink into the ground, who could bring you flowing water? Only the Most Merciful can bring it. Therefore, those who rely on other than Him are in clear misguidance. We have believed in the Most Merciful, submitted to Him, and to Him we shall return."